    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter June 15th with a clear focal point in **Pete Crow-Armstrong**, who commands the day's highest efficiency score (80.98) against Lorenzen. His matchup is optimized by a rare alignment of a favorable pitching profile (4.35 xERA), a 10% boost for leading off, and an environmental edge with wind blowing out. While **Junior Caminero** (70.04) faces a slightly tougher arm in Lauer, he remains a strong start due to his anticipated middle-of-the-order protection and significant SP skill advantage. We are also leaning into **Trea Turner** (63.89) against Gusto; while the opposing arm is respectable, the combination of **Trea Turner**'s aggressive baserunning profile against a pitcher with a slow delivery provides the exact type of stolen base volatility we prize to keep pace in our scarcest category.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved the marginal outfield tier, where **Carson Benge** (48.31) and **Lars Nootbaar** (45.19) barely cleared the threshold over our depth options. **Carson Benge** is a risky play against a high-end arm in Burns, but he earns the start over internal depth like **Luke Raley** or **Nelson Velazquez** due to the efficiency of the lead-off spot. We are carefully monitoring the status of **Junior Caminero**; his score is predicated on the assumed #4 spot, and should the manager shuffle the lineup down, his value would dip toward our \"Marginal\" floor. We remain cautious with **Dillon Dingler** (41.43), whose utility is currently hampered by a dynamic platoon disadvantage; however, in a 5x5 format where we must guard our game caps, we are holding firm with these starters rather than burning capacity on lower-upside bench options like **Josh Smith** or **Casey Schmitt**.",
